## a b s t r a c t
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are prospective photocatalysts for removing pollutants. However, the large size of MOFs results in unsatisfactory photocatalytic performance, thus restricting their further usage. Herein, ultrasmall Ti MOF $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}–\mathsf{M I L}–125(\mathrm{Ti}))$ nanodots (diameter: $\textless10\mathrm{nm}$ ) were prepared on carbon fiber (CF) (diameter: ${\sim}7\upmu\mathrm{m}\dot{}$ based $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ (thickness: ${\sim}20~\mathrm{nm}$ , length: ${\sim}200~\mathrm{nm}$ ) via a facile method and used as an efficient and reusable photocatalyst. The weaved $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth $(0.15{\mathrm{g}}{\mathrm{.}}$ $4\times4~\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) shows good reusability with an easy reusing process. Compared with large size $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) based sample, our well-prepared $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanodots based sample shows the improved surface area $(290.1~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and it can generate more reactive oxygen species (ROS), which enhance removal performance $(81.1\%$ levofloxacin (LVFX), $67.9\%$ acid orange 7 (AO7), $94.3\%$ methylene blue (MB) and $100\%$ $\mathbf{Cr}(\mathbf{\underline{{UI}}}))$ in $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Additionally, the recycling test for 4 cycles indicates high stability. This work highlights the function of easy-recyclable $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanodots-based heterojunctions in wastewater purification.
