## 1. Introduction
The water crisis has become one of the top five global risks [1]. For example, there are $7.05\times10^{5}\mathrm{m}^{3}$ of pharmaceutical wastewater (e.g. levofloxacin (LVFX)), $1.84\times10^{10}~\mathrm{m}^{3}$ of dye wastewater (e.g. acid orange 7 (AO7) and methylene blue (MB)) and $3.34\times10^{6}$ $\mathfrak{m}^{3}$ of heavy metal wastewater (e.g. hexavalent chromium $(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})),$ in 2015 in China [2,3]. In this case, water pollution raises major concerns [4]. For purifying wastewater in a green and efficient way, photocatalysis has drawn much attention as a sunlightdriven technology [5]. Typically, the quality of photocatalyst determines the efficiency of photocatalysis in practical applications. However, most of the reported photocatalysts with satisfactory purification efficiency are in powder form, which is not conducive to recycling. To solve this problem, recently we developed $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ with high photocatalytic performance on carbon fiber (CF) cloth. This CF-based catalyst was used to construct a new device for wastewater purification, and it can efficiently remove $92\%$ rhodamine B (RhB) in the 7th filtering grade under visible light with easy recycling process [6]. However, CF based $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ is limited in the photoabsorption performance. It is favorable to broaden photoabsorption range.
$\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ nanomaterials can be used as an efficient photocatalyst, owing to the large specific surface area and wide-spectrum response [7]. It should be pointed out that large specific surface areas are related to the layered structure, promoting the separation of carriers [8]. Besides, it would greatly enhance the adsorption and photocatalytic ability if narrow bandgap $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ $_{\cdot\sim1.9}$ eV) constructed with other catalysts to form heterojunctions [9]. Most of the traditional photocatalysts show satisfactory photocatalytic efficiency, but they are limited in adsorption performance. Therefore, it is urgent to explore a catalyst with admirable adsorption and photocatalytic synergy effect.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) possess ultrahigh surface areas, permanent porosity, high stability and controllable catalytic properties, which make them be promising adsorbents and catalysts [10]. Besides, some MOFs have been mass-produced already [11,12]. Among these MOFs, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) is a widely researched MOFs owing to it is relatively cheap, nontoxic and stable [13]. It is a prospective candidate in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction [14], water splitting and organic oxidation [15]. Nevertheless, the actual applications of single $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) are still primarily restricted, owing to the unsatisfactory separation efficiency of carriers. For this reason, constructing heterostructure has been addressed owing to the synergistic effect of components that can promote the separation of carriers [16]. Recently, some $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) decorated on semiconductors like $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ [13], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and BiOBr [17] show efficient photocatalytic activities. $\mathsf{B i O B r/N H}_{2}.$ MIL-125(Ti) can efficiently degrade more RhB $(\sim95\%)$ in $100\ \mathrm{min}$ than single BiOBr $(\sim70\%)$ or $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) $(\sim80\%)$ . Nevertheless, no work related to $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) has been reported, and these powder-shaped heterojunctions are limited in the complicated recycling process.
Herein, we report the ternary composite made by $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ nanosheets, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanodots and CF substrate firstly. The diameter of the obtained MOFs is ultrasmall $(<10\mathrm{nm})$ , no work related to such small-sized Ti MOFs has been reported so far. It has been reported that reactive oxygen species (ROS) exhibits limited diffusion distance $(20\mathrm{nm})$ [18]. Thus, ultrasmall nanodots can generate more effective ROS than large-size samples under visible light for photodegrading pollutants. The weaved $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ - MIL-125(Ti) cloth can effectively adsorb and photodegrade LVFX, AO7, MB or $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ .
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Synthesis process
$\mathbf{CF/MoS}_{2}$ : CF was ultrasonically washed in a mixture solution (deionized water, ethanol and acetone; v/v/v, 1:1:1) for $40\ \mathrm{min}$ to thoroughly remove organic residues and surface impurities. ${\mathrm{CF/MoS}}_{2}$ was obtained by a hydrothermal route [19]. 2 mmol $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{6}\mathrm{Mo}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{24}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $30~\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}S$ were dissolved by $70~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. CF was treated at $200~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ with obtained solution, then washed and dried at $60\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C.\ \mathsf M\mathsf{o S}_{2}$ powder was prepared without the CF precursor.
$\mathbf{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti): 7.45 mmol 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}^{\cdot}$ was added into the mixed solution of $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and $22.5~\mathrm{mL}$ N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), followed by adding $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ tetra- $\cdot n$ -butyl titanate and stirring for another $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ [20]. ${\mathrm{CF/MoS}}_{2}$ was treated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ with obtained solution, then washed by DMF and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ . $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) powders were synthesized without ${\mathrm{CF}}/{\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ or CF precursors.
## 2.2. Measurements of photocatalytic performance
The obtained catalysts $(4\times4~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $_{0.15\mathrm{~g}}\cdot$ were tested by removing $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ LVFX $20\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}}.$ ), AO7 $\mathrm{{(10~mg~L^{-1}}}$ ), MB ( $10~\mathrm{mg}$ ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ $(50\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}.$ in the dark to explore adsorption property and under visible light illumination to investigate photocatalytic capacity.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of photocatalysts
The CF-based ternary composite was prepared by hydrothermal-solvothermal-weaving route (Fig. 1). Black CF cloth (Fig. S1a) consists of rough surface fibers (diameter: ${\sim}7~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ (Fig. S1b and c). The cloth becomes blacker after hydrothermal treatment (Fig. 2a). The fiber is coated (Fig. 2b), and the diameter is increased to $11\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ (the inset of Fig. 2b). Fig. 2c reveals that the coating is made by ultrathin nanosheets (thickness: ${\sim}20~\mathrm{nm}$ , length: ${\sim}200~\mathrm{nm}$ ). After solvothermal growth, the color becomes less black (Fig. 2d). At low magnification, there is no obvious difference compared with ${\mathrm{CF/MoS}}_{2}$ (Fig. 2e). Fig. 2f shows that there are thousands of nanodots with a size $\mathsf{o f}\mathsf{<}10\ \mathrm{nm}$ on the nanosheet. $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) powder is dark brown, which made by nanoparticles and nanosheets (Fig. $2g$ and h). MOFs in $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) powder displays a much larger diameter $\cdot{\sim}150~\mathrm{nm})$ (Fig. 2i) compared with that in $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125 (Ti).
The $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) heterostructure was peeled from CF substrate, and then explored by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Obviously, there are many stacked nanosheets (Fig. 3a). Close examination via HR-TEM (Fig. 3b) confirmed that many nanodots $(<10\ \mathrm{nm})$ were coated on the surface of the nanosheets. Besides, these stacked nanosheets with the layer spacing of $\sim0.6$ nm (Fig. 3c), which corresponds to S-Mo-S layers [19]. Elemental mapping analysis was applied to explore the distribution of each element (Fig. ${3\mathrm{d}}{-\mathrm{g}\mathrm{\cdot}}$ . The images suggest that Mo, S and Ti elements are evenly loaded on the nanosheets, confirming the satisfactory construction of catalyst. The decoration of ultrasmall nanodots on large ultrathin nanosheets may provide lots of active sites, which is beneficial for removing pollutants.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of obtained catalysts were displayed in Fig. 4a. Pure CF displays a broad peak at $\sim26^{\circ}$ , relating to the graphite phase [21]. After growing $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ on CF, ${\mathrm{CF/MoS}}_{2}$ exhibits another three peaks at $13.80^{\circ}$ , $32.66^{\circ}$ and $57.74^{\circ}$ , attributing to hexagonal $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ (JCPDS card No. 37–1492) [22]. After decorating $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanodots on $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ nanosheets, ${\sf M o S}_{2}/{\sf N H}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) powder show a new peak from $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) $(6.78^{\circ})$ , which is similar to the simulated $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) of the previous report (Fig. S2) [23]. $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) exhibits three peaks from $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ $\cdot13.80^{\circ}\lambda$ , $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) $(6.78^{\circ})$ and CF $(\sim26^{\circ})$ , indicating the good formation of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) on fiber.
The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and ternary composite were measured. Fig. 4b proves the existence of N, O, Ti, Mo, C and S elements in the composite. In Ti 2p spectra (Fig. 4c), $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) shows two main peaks at 458.9 and $464.7\mathrm{eV}$ , relating to Ti $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ti $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ [17]. Ti $2{\tt p}$ peaks of composite exhibit a positive shift, resulting from the transfer of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ to $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ [24]. For Mo 3d spectra (Fig. 4d), $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ shows two peaks at $232.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $229.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , relating to Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ . Compared with $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) displays higher binding energies (232.6 and $229.4{\mathrm{~eV}}^{\cdot}$ . The slight shift of binding energy value suggests the tight connection between $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ in the composite [25].
The BET surface areas and porous structure of as-synthesized catalysts were explored by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption test. $\mathsf{C F/}$ $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ shows type IV isotherm (Fig. 4e) with low surface area and large pore width (Table 1). CF/ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) displays the typical type I isotherm with the highest surface area, total pore volume and relatively small pore width, relating to its typical porous structure [26]. For $\mathrm{MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{(Ti),}$ it exhibits hybrid type I/IV isotherms, which should be related to the existence of micropores and mesopores. The ternary composite exhibits improved surface area, which enhances adsorption capacity and catalytic efficiency normally. Besides, narrow pore size distribution (Fig. 4f) of asprepared catalysts implies good homogeneity of pores.
The optical performance of obtained photocatalysts was tested by UV–Vis-NIR spectra (Fig. 5a). $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ displays the strongest adsorption without an obvious adsorption edge. ${\mathrm{CF}}/{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ exhibits broad absorptions with an increased absorption tail, owing to scattering-absorption of the CF substrate [6,27]. $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) possesses a clear edge at $\sim500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , relating to ligand-based absorption [13]. ${\sf M o S}_{2}/{\sf N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) displays a wide absorption, while $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) displays slightly enhanced photoabsorption performance, probably relating to the scattering-absorption by CF [6]. The bandgap energies $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ were obtained from plots of $(\alpha h\nu)^{2}$ for $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) [13] and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ [28] vs. photon energy $(h\nu)$ . The calculated $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ values of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ are $2.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 5b) and $1.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S3), respectively. In addition, the flat band potentials of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) were confirmed by combining the Mott-Schottky plot and the equation of $\scriptstyle\frac{1}{C_{S C}}=\frac{2}{e\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}}\left(E-E_{F B}-\frac{K T}{e}\right)$ [29], where $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{s c}}$ is the capacitance of the space charge region, e is the dielectric constant of the semiconductor, $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{0}}$ is the permittivity of free space, k is Boltzmann’s constant, T is the temperature, $E$ and $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{FB}}}$ is the applied potential and flatband potential, respectively. So the $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{FB}}}$ value can be determined from the extrapolation to $1/C^{2}=0$ . The $E$ values of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) are $-0.67$ and $-0.63\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 5c-d), respectively. When the value of $\mathrm{KT}/\mathrm{e}=0.0257$ , the $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{FB}}}$ of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) are calculated to be $-0.70$ and $-0.66$ eV vs. $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ (-0.50 and 0.46 eV vs. NHE), respectively. The MS plot slops of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) are positive, suggesting that they are $n$ -type semiconductors. Therefore, according to the formula of $E_{\mathrm{CB}}\approx E_{\mathrm{FB}}-0.2$ [30], the $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ values of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-125(Ti) are calculated to be $-0.70$ and $-0.66\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. According to the formula of $E_{\mathrm{VB}}=E_{\mathrm{CB}}+E_{\mathrm{g}}$ , the corresponding $\scriptstyle{E_{\mathrm{VB}}}$ of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ are 1.84 and $1.00\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively.
The photoelectrochemical technique was applied to test interface charge separation efficiency. The working electrode was constructed by ITO glass with catalysts, and the transient current was explored for several continuous on–off cycles at $0.5~\mathrm{V}$ with 0.2 M ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ solution (Fig. 5e). Both four photoanodes showed a steep inclination of the photocurrent with visible light is on/off, suggesting high stability. ${\mathrm{CF}}/{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ shows an exceptionally low photocurrent density $(\sim0.8~\upmu\mathrm{A}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , attributing to the quick recombination of carriers. CF $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) exhibits obvious photocurrent response $(\sim2.4~\upmu\mathrm{A}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) powder displays enhanced current density $(\sim3.1\ \upmu\mathrm{A}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ . It should be noted that the ternary composite shows the highest photocurrent density $(\sim4.6~\upmu\mathrm{A}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ), confirming it is an effective approach to generate and transfer the photoinduced carriers. Besides, the diameter of the Nyquist semicircle for the ternary composite in electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) spectra is smaller than that of other as-prepared catalysts (Fig. 5f), indicating the lowest resistance of $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) [31,32]. Thus, rapid transfer of photoexcited carriers between three components promotes the carriers’ separation and improves the photodegrading efficiency.
## 3.2. Photocatalytic properties
Various pollutants were discharged to the water environment, including antibiotics, dyes and heavy metals. To explore the pollutants removal activity of as-prepared catalysts, LVFX, AO7, MB and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ were used to detect adsorption and catalytic performance of obtained cloths and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) powder (Fig. 6a). Leeman Prodigy inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) results show that there are $0.1\mathrm{~g~MoS}_{2}$ and $0.6\mathrm{~g~NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) in $_\mathrm{~1~g~}$ original $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti). Therefore, $0.105\mathrm{~g~MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) is applied for comparison. With CF cloth as the catalyst, almost no pollutants were removed, suggesting that CF cloth has no adsorption or photodegradation activity. After growing $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ on CF, ${\mathrm{CF}}/{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ cloth shows enhanced adsorption efficiency for LVFX $(23.0\%)$ , AO7 $(18.7\%)$ , MB $(30.2\%)$ and $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ $(19.4\%)$ in $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , owing to the mesoporous with the average pore width of $29.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Table 1). $\mathrm{CF}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth can also adsorb more LVFX $(26.8\%)$ , AO7 $(20.3\%)$ , MB $(30.6\%)$ and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ $(21.2\%)$ in $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , owing to the growth of MOFs nanodots on CF inevitably increases surface area from 0.33 to $486.6\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Table 1) [33]. ${\sf M o S}_{2}/{\sf N H}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) shows relatively high adsorption efficiency for LVFX $(30.9\%)$ , AO7 $(24.8\%)$ , MB $(36.8\%)$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\left(30.7\%\right)$ . It is worth noting that the ternary composite displays the highest adsorption capacity for LVFX $(37.8\%)$ , AO7 $(30.9\%)$ , MB $(44.8\%)$ and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ $(38.2\%)$ in $60\mathrm{{min}}$ , ascribing to the high specific surface area, mesoporous and microporous structure. Besides, the molecular sizes of LVFX, AO7, MB and $\mathbf{Cr}({\underline{{\mathbb{W}}}})$ were calculated by Materials Studio to be 1.6, 1.4, 1.5 and $0.7{\mathrm{nm}}$ , respectively (Fig. 6b), which were much smaller than the pore width of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) $(4.9\mathrm{nm})$ ), facilitating the penetration of pollutant molecules. Thus, the superior adsorption of the ternary composite should be ascribed to two aspects: (1) The decoration of ultrasmall $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanodots on ${\mathrm{CF/MoS}}_{2}$ . After decorating $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) on ${\mathrm{CF/MoS}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) shows enhanced surface area $(290.1~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Table 1), providing active sites for binding pollutants; (2) The exists of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloths. Large-sized $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ nanosheets not only promote the growth of nanodots but also provide mesoporous to promote the free penetration of pollutants into the catalyst.
Under visible light illumination, ${\mathrm{CF}}/{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ cloth can remove $53.8\%$ LVFX, $22.8\%$ AO7, $54.9~\%$ MB and $31.8\%\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Fig. 6c-f) since $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ can be excited with an $E\mathbf{g}$ value of $\sim1.9\mathrm{eV}$
Considering that the as-prepared cloth can be easily floated on the aqueous solution, the generated $\mathbf{e}^{-}$ in $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ can react with $0_{2}$ in the air or pollutant solution to produce $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ owing to the $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ is more negative than $E^{0}(0_{2}/\cdot0_{2}^{-})$ (-0.046 eV vs NHE) [34]. The produced $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ further oxidize LVFX, AO7 and MB, while $\mathsf{e}^{-}$ reduces $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . However, the narrow bandgap of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ results in relatively high recombination of $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , thereby limiting the degradation efficiency. CF $/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth can remove $47.1\%$ LVFX, $29.1\%$ AO7, $61\%$ MB and $45.1\%\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in $120\mathrm{min}$ . Similarly, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) can produce radicals $(\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ and $\mathbf{e}^{-}$ ) due to the narrow bandgap and negative $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ , promoting the removal of pollutants. ${\sf M o S}_{2}/{\sf N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) shows relatively high removal efficiency for LVFX $(67.7\%)$ , AO7 $(48.5\%)$ , MB $(75.7\%)$ and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ $(76.3\%)$ . C $\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth exhibits the best removal performance for LVFX $(81.1\%)$ , AO7 $(67.9\%)$ , MB $(94.3\%)$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ $(100\%)$ . These facts strongly prove that $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth is capable of removing various pollutants from wastewater.
To further investigate the effects of initial pH on reducing $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ by $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) ternary cloth, the initial $\mathsf{p H}$ of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ solution was adjusted to 8.2, 7.0, 4.1 and 2.1 by using $0.1\mathrm{~M~HCl}/$ NaOH at room temperature. The removal efficiency decreased from $100\%$ to $50.3\%$ with the $\mathsf{p H}$ increased from 2.1 to 8.2 (Fig. 7a). It should be related to that $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ usually exists in $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}^{2-}$ under acidic conditions and $\mathsf{C r O}_{4}^{2-}$ under alkaline conditions. Since ${\boldsymbol{E}}^{0}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}^{2-}/$ $\mathsf{C r}^{3+}$ is $1.36\:\mathrm{eV}$ , while $E^{0}$ of $\mathrm{CrO_{4}^{2-}/C r(O H)_{3}}$ is $-0.13\mathrm{eV}$ vs NHE, $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}^{2-}$ is easier to be reduced [35]. Furthermore, the produced $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ under alkaline conditions will further cover the active sites of the catalyst, inhibiting further $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction. Besides, the effect of the water source was further explored by using deionized water, Jingyue lake water and tap water in the Donghua University as the three different water sources to obtain initial $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ solution at pH of 2.1 (Fig. 7b). The ternary cloth can reduce $73\%\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in tap water after $120\ \mathrm{min}$ , which is much lower than that $(100\%)$ in deionized water. This phenomenon should be related to that there are various inorganic ions in tap water, and some of these inorganic ion species hurt photocatalytic activity [36]. When lake water was applied to obtain the initial $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ solution, $\mathrm{CF}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth can remove $46\%\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in $120\mathrm{min}$ . It should be noted that the organic matters can efficiently react with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ generated by the catalyst, promoting the separation of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ and $\mathsf{e}^{-}$ , further accelerating $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal. However, organic matters may shade and compete with free radicals [37]. Therefore, the asprepared ternary composite exhibits the highest $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal efficiency with deionized water as the water source.
The free radical trapping test was carried out to explore the role of each free radical in removing LVFX over $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) cloth (Fig. 8a). The LVFX degradation efficiency slightly decreased with the addition of ${\sf A g N O}_{3}$ or ammonium oxalate (AO) from $81.1\%$ to $76.4\%$ or $72.9\%$ (Fig. 8b). While after putting benzoquinone (BQ) or isopropyl alcohol (IPA), photocatalytic efficiency of cloth drastically dropped to $37.7\%$ or $60.6\%$ , suggesting $.0_{2}^{-}$ and OH are main radicals. In addition, the results of electron spin resonance (ESR) exhibit clear signals of $\mathsf{D M P O-}.0_{2}^{-}$ (Fig. 8c) and DMPO-OH (Fig. 8d) under the light, confirming the critical roles of $.0_{2}^{-}$ and OH in LVFX removal.
The stability of ternary composite was explored by a four-cycle test in degrading LVFX under visible light. The photocatalytic efficiency shows a slightly downward trend from $81.1\%$ to $73.4\%$ after four cycles (Fig. 8e), verifying the good stability of ternary cloth in degrading LVFX. This slightly downward trend should be related to two aspects. One is that many pollutants were absorbed on cloth, leading to incomplete desorption of LVFX. The other is the photocorrosion or photodissolution that occurred on the surface of the photocatalyst [38,39]. Additionally, the XRD characterizations of the used cloth after four cycles test are almost identical to that of the original one, as shown in Fig. 8f, suggesting the good stability of C $\mathrm{\bar{F}/M o S_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth.
## 3.3. Photocatalytic and degradation mechanism
Based on the above results, $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) displays good photocatalytic activity. To further illustrate the advantages of $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti), we have compared the photocatalytic performances towards LVFX, AO7, MB and $\operatorname{Cr}({\underline{{\mathbb{W}}}})$ by different photocatalytic materials (Table S1). Compared with other related publications in Table S1, $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) displays relatively good photocatalytic removal efficiency toward LVFX, AO7, MB and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ with the easy recycling process. There are chiefly four reasons for the superior removal efficiency: (Ⅰ) strong and wide absorption photoabsorption range from 400 to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 5a); (Ⅱ) enhanced adsorption efficiency after growing ultrathin nanosheets and ultrasmall nanodots, resulting in the large specific surface area of $290.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Table 1); (III) effective separation of carriers since well-matched band positions of components [40,41] and $(\mathbb{X})$ effective generation of ROS under visible light irradiation.
Photocatalytic removal mechanism from $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL125(Ti) is proposed as follow (Fig. 9). In the dark, due to the open channels and high specific surface area, $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth shows high adsorption and diffusion of pollutant molecules. Under visible light irradiation, both $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) could generate photoexcited carriers [Eq. (1) and (2)]. Since the $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ is more negative, $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ could flow from $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ into $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-125(Ti), then into conductive CF [Eq. (3)]. Resulting from the more negative $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ than $E^{0}(0_{2}/\cdot0_{2}^{-})$ [34], $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ in components could further reduce $0_{2}$ to $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ [Eq. (4)
and (5) ]. Besides, the photo-generated and injected $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ leads to the transfer of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ to $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ [Eq. (6)]. Then, the adsorbed $0_{2}$ were reduced to $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ through the negative charge transportation in the Ti-O clusters, owing to the strong reduction ability of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ (- 1.37 V vs. NHE) [17]. Correspondingly, the $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ is oxidized to $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ relating to the presence of $\mathrm{Ti^{3+}\mathrm{-}T i^{4+}}$ intervalence electron transfer [Eq. (7)]. Part of generated $\cdot_{\mathrm{{O}_{2}^{-}}}$ can further produce OH [Eq. (8)- (10)]. At the same time, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) were flow to $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ owing to the more positive $\cal{E}_{\mathrm{VB}}$ [Eq. (11)]. The $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ accumulated on VB of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ is more negative than $E^{0}\left(\cdot\mathrm{OH}/0\mathrm{H}^{-}\right)$ [42], suggesting no OH was produced by $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ in VB of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ or $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti). The produced active radicals show strong reduction and oxidation capability, promoting degradation of LVFX, AO7, MB or $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ [Eq. (12)].
