The produced intermediates in photodegradation reactions were also detected by liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LCMS). The LVFX removal pathway usually includes piperazine ring oxidation, quinolone moieties transformation and decarboxylation [43,44]. According to the signal of products, three possible removal routes were proposed (Fig. 10). For the first path, P1 was produced by LVFX demethylation, and it can be further decarboxylated to form $P2$ and then defluorinated and hydroxylated to form P3 [45]. The second reaction mechanism is the piperazine ring broken and further oxidized into aldehyde products (P4 and P5). Then, P6 and P7 could be formed by decarboxylation and defluorination [44]. In path III, P8, P9 and $P10$ were related to decarboxylation, piperazinyl group removal and defluorination. Finally, these intermediate products may be broken down into small molecules.
## 4. Conclusions
The ultrasmall $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanodots (diameter: $<10~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) were successfully synthesized on the surface of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ nanosheets grown on CF. The as-prepared $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth has four obvious advantages. The first is strong and wide absorption photoabsorption range from 400 to $800\mathrm{nm}$ . The second is high adsorption ability. In the dark, $\mathsf{T}/\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth can efficiently adsorb $37.8\%$ LVFX, $30.9\%$ AO7, $44.8\%$ MB and $38.2\%$ Cr (VI). High adsorption ability should result from the high surface area $(290.1~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{\bf~g}^{-1}.$ ) after decorating ultrasmall $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanodots and mesoporous structure provided by $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ to promote the free penetration of pollutants into the catalyst. The third is high photocatalytic activity. Under visible-light irradiation, ${\mathrm{CF}}/{\mathrm{MoS}_{2}}/$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth can photocatalytically eliminate $81.1\%$ LVFX, $67.9\%$ AO7, $94.3~\%$ MB and $100\%\ \mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . High removal efficiency should result from double-component synergistic effect and effective ROS. The last is good stability with a simple recycling process. The macroscopical $\mathrm{CF/MoS_{2}/N H_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) cloth with an area of $4\times4~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ can be easily recycled and reused, and there are no obvious changes in their morphologies and photocatalytic activity after four-cycle tests. This study brings a novel idea of construction and application of easy-recyclable and effective MOFs nanodots-based catalyst with the synergistic effect of adsorption and photocatalysis.
